<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yining</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ghulja, Kulja, Gulja</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yining, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> as Ghulja, is a city in the Ili valley of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, near the border with Kazakhstan. In the Qing period it was a trading town of the Ili region, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> occupied from 1871 to 1881. In November 1944 rebels took the city and proclaimed the Second East Turkestan Republic there, and in 1997 it was the scene of protests suppressed by the authorities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yining.jpg" alt="Yining" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Akira CA · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batul_Mosque_of_Yining.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
